>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Cost Explained"} badge="Research-Backed" author={author} dates={dates} label="Our standards">
      <Fragment slot="title">Why Is Protein Powder So Expensive?</Fragment>
      For anyone staring at a $60 tub and wondering where the money goes. We break the price into its parts and show which parts are worth paying for.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#what-drives-price">What Drives the Price</a>
      <a href="#why-whey-expensive">Why Whey Costs More Now</a>
      <a href="#isolate-plant">Isolate, Plant and Specialty Types</a>
      <a href="#brand-bag-store">Brand, Bag Size and Store</a>
      <a href="#worth-it">Is Expensive Powder Worth It?</a>
      <a href="#cheapest-way">The Cheapest Way per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#red-flags">Price Red Flags</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Protein powder is expensive because you are paying for milk that has been filtered down to pure protein, then for the bag, the brand and the store, and each step adds cost. A normal powder costs $1.80 to $2.80 for every 25 g of protein. Isolates, plant blends, small bags and celebrity brands push past $3. This page explains each cost driver, when the premium buys something real and how to pay under $1.50.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Is Protein Powder So Expensive? The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Protein powder costs what it does because concentrated protein is an expensive ingredient, and then four more costs stack on top of it: extra processing for isolates and specialty proteins, small packaging, brand marketing and retail margin. Across the 104 priced powders in our label database, checked in November 2026, the median tub is about $59 and the median cost per 25 g of protein is $2.26. The cheapest quarter cost under $1.80 per 25 g; the dearest quarter cost over $2.78.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Where the Price Lands (104 powders, Nov 2026)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$2.26</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Median cost per 25 g</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$1.34</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Casein median per 25 g</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">$2.43</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Whey isolate median per 25 g</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">$3.17</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Bone broth median per 25 g</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The useful question is not whether a powder is expensive but what the extra money buys. Some premiums are real: isolate has less lactose, egg and beef are dairy-free animal proteins, and a sport certification matters if you are drug-tested. Others are mostly story: a famous founder, a stand-up pouch, a superfood blend that adds a few cents of greens and a dollar of margin. If you cannot name what the premium pays for, the cheaper tub is the better buy. For the full price bands by type and eighteen real tubs worked out, see our <a href="/how-much-does-protein-powder-cost/" class="text-[#FF4500] font-semibold hover:underline">guide to how much protein powder costs</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-drives-price" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Actually Drives the Price of Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Seven things decide what a tub costs, and they are not equally important. The protein source and the bag size move the price most. The brand and the store come next. Testing and organic or grass-fed sourcing add a little, sometimes less than shoppers assume. The table below ranks them by how much they typically move cost per 25 g in our database, and says whether the premium buys you anything you would notice.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost driver</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical effect on cost per 25 g</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Does the premium buy something real?</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein source (concentrate vs isolate vs plant blend vs bone broth)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Large: about $1.30 to $3.20 by type at the median</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often yes: lactose, calories, dairy-free</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bag or tub size (1 lb pouch vs 5 lb tub)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Large: bulk is usually 15-30% cheaper per gram</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No: same powder, less packaging per gram</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Brand and marketing (influencer, celebrity, DTC)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Medium to large</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Rarely</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Where you buy it (warehouse club vs specialty store)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Medium: the same tub can differ 20%+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Add-ins (greens, collagen, adaptogens, probiotics)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Medium, and they lower protein per scoop</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Seldom at the dose used</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Organic or grass-fed sourcing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Small to medium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A farming standard, not more protein</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Third-party testing (NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Small: certified powders span about $1.25-$3.97</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, if you are tested or cautious</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two things in that table surprise most people. First, certification is cheap relative to everything else: the NSF Certified for Sport powders we track range from $1.25 to $3.97 per 25 g, roughly the same spread as uncertified powders, so a certified tub is not automatically a pricey one. Our <a href="/best-nsf-certified-protein-powder/" class="text-[#FF4500] font-semibold hover:underline">NSF certified protein powder guide</a> lists the certified tubs that are also good value.
          </p>

          <p>
            Second, packaging matters more than ingredients for many direct-to-consumer brands. A 15-serving pouch sold mainly through a brand's website carries the cost of shipping single orders, paid advertising and subscription discounts that the list price was set high enough to absorb. Of the 20 powders in our database that cost $3 or more per 25 g, more than half are small tubs or bags of 15 to 30 servings sold mostly by the brand itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            Everything else on the list is a smaller nudge. Add-ins are the sneakiest, because they raise the price and lower the protein in the same scoop, which hits cost per 25 g twice. Organic and grass-fed sourcing raise the price of the raw ingredient, but the protein you get is nutritionally close to conventional whey.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-whey-expensive" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Why Is Whey Protein So Expensive Now?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Whey is expensive because it is a by-product with a limited supply and a fast-growing list of buyers. Whey comes from cheesemaking: the liquid left after milk is turned into cheese is filtered, concentrated and dried. How much whey exists depends on how much cheese is made, not on how many people want protein shakes, so supply cannot jump just because demand does.
          </p>

          <p>
            Demand has jumped. High-protein yogurts, bars, ready-to-drink shakes, cereals and coffee drinks all compete for the same whey protein concentrate and isolate that tubs of powder use, and food makers buy it by the truckload. Industry and dairy-market reports through 2024 and 2025 described wholesale whey protein prices climbing as that demand grew. We do not quote a wholesale figure here because those prices move weekly and are sold by subscription, but the direction showed up in retail: several big brands raised list prices or trimmed the number of servings in a tub during that period.
          </p>

          <p>
            Trimmed servings are worth watching for. When a brand holds the tub price steady but cuts the contents from 2.5 lb to 2 lb, or keeps the weight but shrinks the scoop so the label claims more servings with less protein in each, the price per 25 g rises without the sticker changing. This is why we compare powders on cost per 25 g of protein, not on tub price. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-is-a-scoop-of-protein-powder/" class="text-[#FF4500] font-semibold hover:underline">how much is in a scoop of protein powder</a> shows how scoop sizes differ between brands and why the same 'serving' can hold 20 g or 30 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            Despite all that, standard whey is still one of the cheaper protein types we track. Whey concentrate has a median of $1.91 per 25 g and whey blends $1.95. The whey premium shows up mostly in isolates and hydrolysates, where more milk goes into each kilo of finished powder.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Compare the protein, not the tub.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Divide the price by servings, divide that by grams of protein per serving, and multiply by 25. Under $1.50 is cheap, $1.50 to $2.25 is normal, $2.25 to $3.00 is premium, and over $3.00 needs a reason you can name.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="isolate-plant" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Why Do Isolate, Plant and Specialty Powders Cost More?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Isolate costs more because it takes more milk to make. Concentrate is filtered until it is roughly 70 to 80 percent protein; isolate is filtered further, to about 90 percent, which strips out most of the lactose and fat and leaves less finished powder from the same starting liquid. In our database the median whey isolate costs $2.43 per 25 g against $1.91 for concentrate, a gap of about 50 cents. Hydrolysate, which is pre-digested with enzymes, adds another processing step and sits at $2.50.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plant powders are the widest-ranging type we track. Plain single-source pea is the cheapest protein in the database, with Naked Pea at $0.71 per 25 g in the 5 lb bag, because pea protein is made at large scale and sold without much branding. Organic multi-source blends with greens, probiotics or superfoods run past $3, and one protein-and-greens powder reaches $6.25 per 25 g because each serving holds only 10 g of protein. The plant blend median is $2.51.
          </p>

          <p>
            The specialty animal proteins cost more for different reasons. Egg white powder (median $2.13) starts from a pricier raw material than whey. Beef isolate ($1.93 median, wide range) is a niche product made at lower volume. Bone broth protein is the most expensive common type at a $3.17 median, because the raw material yields relatively little protein and the products are sold as wellness items with premium margins. Its amino acid profile is also weaker for muscle building than whey, so you pay more and get less of the leucine that drives muscle protein synthesis.
          </p>

          <p>
            Casein is the cheap surprise: a $1.34 median per 25 g, the lowest of any type with several products priced. It is a dairy protein made at scale and sold mostly in big tubs, and it has less hype attached. If you like a thick, slow-digesting shake, it is the best value in the dairy aisle. For a side-by-side of the two dairy options, read our <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/" class="text-[#FF4500] font-semibold hover:underline">whey vs whey isolate comparison</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="brand-bag-store" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Much Do the Brand, Bag Size and Store Add?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Together, brand, bag size and store can double the price of the same kind of protein. Bag size is the easiest to see. A 5 lb tub usually costs 15 to 30 percent less per gram than a 2 lb tub of the same powder: Naked prices its 5 lb whey at about $28 a pound against $38 for the 1 lb bag. The protein is the same; you are paying less for plastic, shipping and shelf space per gram.
          </p>

          <p>
            The store matters almost as much. Gold Standard 100% Whey was $79.99 for 3.96 lb at Target in our database and $124.99 for 5.05 lb at The Vitamin Shoppe, which is $1.44 and $1.76 per 25 g before promotions. Warehouse clubs, supercenters and Amazon tend to charge least for name brands; gyms, pharmacies and specialty stores tend to charge most. Store brands undercut both: our <a href="/best-costco-protein-powder/" class="text-[#FF4500] font-semibold hover:underline">Costco protein powder guide</a> and <a href="/best-aldi-protein-powder/" class="text-[#FF4500] font-semibold hover:underline">Aldi protein powder guide</a> show house brands that beat the name brands on the same shelf.
          </p>

          <p>
            Brand premiums are the hardest to justify. Influencer and celebrity brands spend heavily on social media, sponsorships and founder-led launches, and that spending is in the price. Some of these brands make good powders with clean labels, and paying a little more for a flavor you will actually finish is reasonable. But a brand premium buys no extra protein, and the gap can be large: a premium brand pouch and a store-brand whey with similar labels can differ by $1.50 or more per 25 g.
          </p>

          <p>
            Subscriptions blur the picture. Many brands list a high one-off price so their subscribe-and-save discount looks generous. Before subscribing, work out cost per 25 g at the subscription price and compare it with the bulk tub of a rival at its normal price. Often the rival still wins.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="worth-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is Expensive Protein Powder Worth It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Expensive protein powder is worth it only when the extra money buys something you specifically need. For most people who drink one shake a day after training and digest dairy fine, a mid-priced whey concentrate or blend at $1.50 to $2.25 per 25 g delivers the same muscle-building amino acids as a $3.50 pouch. Your muscles cannot tell which brand paid for the influencer campaign.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When paying more makes sense</h3>

          <p>
            Pay for isolate if lactose gives you gas or bloating, or if you are cutting and every gram of fat and carbohydrate counts. Pay for NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport if you are a tested athlete or want independent screening for banned substances; the premium is modest. Pay for a dairy-free animal protein such as egg white or beef isolate if you avoid dairy but want a complete protein without pea's earthy taste. And pay a little more for a flavor you enjoy, because a cheap tub you abandon halfway is the most expensive protein there is.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When it doesn't</h3>

          <p>
            Do not pay extra for greens, superfoods, adaptogens or probiotics blended into a protein powder. The doses are usually small, the protein per scoop drops to make room for them, and you can buy a dedicated supplement if you want one. Do not pay a large premium for 'grass-fed' alone; it is a farming standard, not a nutrition upgrade. And do not assume a high price means cleaner or safer powder: heavy-metal results from independent testers have not tracked price neatly, which is why our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/" class="text-[#FF4500] font-semibold hover:underline">guide to protein powder without heavy metals</a> ranks by test results rather than by price.
          </p>

          <p>
            Also check how much powder you actually need. If you already eat enough protein from food, one scoop a day is plenty and a cheap tub lasts a month or more. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-protein-powder-per-day/" class="text-[#FF4500] font-semibold hover:underline">how much protein powder to take per day</a> helps size it, which matters because the cheapest powder is the one you do not overbuy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cheapest-way" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Is the Cheapest Way to Buy Protein Powder per 25 g?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The cheapest way to buy protein powder is a large tub of a plain whey, casein or pea powder from a store brand or bulk seller, bought on sale. Each of those choices strips out one of the cost drivers above, and stacking them regularly lands below $1.25 per 25 g.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Move</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost driver it removes</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical saving</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Buy the 5 lb tub of a flavor you already like</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Small-bag packaging</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">15-30% per gram</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Choose a store brand (Kirkland, Elevation at Aldi, Equate)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brand marketing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often $0.50+ per 25 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Pick concentrate or a blend over isolate if dairy is fine</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Extra filtering</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $0.50 per 25 g at the median</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Buy plain pea or casein instead of a plant blend</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Add-ins and niche sourcing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Up to half</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Shop warehouse clubs, supercenters or Amazon</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Retail margin</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-20% on name brands</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Time it for Black Friday, Prime events or January sales</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">List-price markup</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20-30% off</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Store brands deserve a closer look than they usually get. Kirkland Signature whey at Costco and Elevation at Aldi are made by contract manufacturers that also make name-brand powders, and their labels compare well on protein per scoop. Our <a href="/kirkland-protein-powder-review/" class="text-[#FF4500] font-semibold hover:underline">Kirkland protein powder review</a> checks its label line by line.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bulk sellers are the other route. Unflavored bulk bags of whey concentrate, whey isolate or pea from bulk-ingredient brands cost far less per gram than flavored retail tubs, and you can flavor them with cocoa, coffee or fruit you already buy. Our <a href="/best-bulk-protein-powder/" class="text-[#FF4500] font-semibold hover:underline">bulk protein powder guide</a> lists the big bags worth buying, and the <a href="/best-cheap-protein-powder/" class="text-[#FF4500] font-semibold hover:underline">best cheap protein powder</a> ranking covers flavored tubs that still come in under $1.50 per 25 g.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="red-flags" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Are the Red Flags on a Pricey Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The biggest red flag is a high price with less protein per scoop than the category norm. A whey powder should give about 20 to 27 g of protein per serving; a plant powder about 20 to 25 g. If a premium tub gives 10 to 15 g and fills the rest of the scoop with greens, fiber or 'proprietary blends', you are paying more for less of the thing you came for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for proprietary blends that hide amounts, for 'serving' sizes that are oddly small so the tub can claim more servings, and for price-per-serving claims that compare a small serving against a rival's full one. Be wary of amino spiking too: a few cheap brands have historically added free amino acids such as glycine or taurine to inflate the protein number, which is why a third-party tested label is worth something at the budget end, not just the premium end.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, be skeptical of sales that are not sales. Some brands raise the list price before a promotion so the discount looks deep. Keep a note of what your usual powder costs per 25 g, and judge every deal against that number rather than against the crossed-out price.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging value by the tub price</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A $25 tub can be worse value than a $60 one if it holds fewer, smaller servings. Always divide price by servings, then by grams of protein per serving, then multiply by 25. That single number makes a pouch, a tub and a bulk bag directly comparable, and it exposes shrunken scoops immediately.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying isolate prices when dairy is fine</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Isolate costs about 50 cents more per 25 g at the median because it takes more milk to make. If lactose does not bother you and you are not counting every gram of fat, a whey concentrate or blend gives you the same amino acids for less. Save isolate for when it solves a real problem.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the extras with the protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Greens, collagen, adaptogens and probiotics raise the price and cut protein per scoop, so cost per 25 g rises twice. The doses are usually too small to matter. If you want one of those supplements, buy it separately at a useful dose and keep your protein powder plain and cheap.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying 5 lb of an untried flavor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bulk is the biggest saving available, but only if you finish the tub. A 5 lb tub of a flavor you dislike becomes the most expensive protein you ever bought. Try a sample, a small tub or a store-brand single first, then buy big once you know you will drink it every day.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming expensive means safer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Price does not track purity neatly. Independent heavy-metal and label-accuracy testing has flagged premium brands as well as cheap ones. If safety is your reason for paying more, look for NSF Certified for Sport, Informed Sport or published test results, which are available at every price point.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Why is protein powder so expensive?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Concentrated protein is a costly ingredient, and processing, packaging, marketing and retail margin all stack on top. Isolates and specialty proteins need more raw material per kilo, small pouches cost more per gram than big tubs, and influencer brands carry heavy marketing costs. A normal powder costs $1.80 to $2.80 per 25 g of protein; much above $3 usually means you are paying for a brand or extras.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Why is whey protein so expensive now?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Whey is a by-product of cheesemaking, so supply depends on cheese output, while demand from bars, yogurts and ready-to-drink shakes has grown quickly. Industry reports through 2024 and 2025 described wholesale whey prices rising, and several brands raised list prices or trimmed servings per tub. Standard whey concentrate is still among the cheaper types at about $1.91 per 25 g.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is expensive protein powder better?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not for muscle building. A mid-priced whey supplies the same amino acids, including leucine, as a premium one. Expensive powder can be better for a specific need: isolate for lactose sensitivity, a sport certification for tested athletes, or egg and beef for dairy-free diets. If you cannot name what the premium pays for, it is probably buying marketing rather than nutrition.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is a good price for protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Under $1.50 per 25 g of protein is cheap, $1.50 to $2.25 is normal and $2.25 to $3.00 is premium. Over $3.00 should buy something specific. Work it out by dividing the tub price by servings, then by grams of protein per serving, and multiplying by 25. The median across our 104 priced powders was $2.26 in November 2026.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Why is plant protein powder more expensive than whey?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Plain pea protein is actually one of the cheapest proteins we track, as low as $0.71 per 25 g in bulk. Plant powders look expensive because many are organic multi-source blends with greens, probiotics or superfoods, small serving sizes and premium branding. The plant blend median is $2.51 per 25 g. Buy single-source pea and flavor it yourself to save.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is store-brand protein powder as good as name brands?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Often yes. Store brands such as Kirkland at Costco and Elevation at Aldi are made by contract manufacturers that also produce name-brand powders, and their labels compare well on protein per scoop and ingredients. They cost less mainly because they skip brand advertising. Check the label for protein per serving and any third-party testing, exactly as you would with a name brand.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>When is protein powder cheapest to buy?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Black Friday and Cyber Monday, Amazon's summer and fall deal events, January fitness sales and brand anniversary sales usually bring the lowest prices of the year, often 20 to 30 percent off. Combine a sale with a big tub of a flavor you already like and a store or bulk brand, and you can regularly get below $1.25 per 25 g of protein.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

