        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 md:p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Protein powder is expensive because concentrated protein costs money to make, and then bag size, brand, store and add-ins stack on top. A normal powder lands at $1.80 to $2.80 per 25 g. Isolate, a sport certification or a dairy-free animal protein can be worth a premium; a famous founder, a pouch and a spoonful of greens usually are not.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            To pay less, buy a big tub of a plain whey, casein or pea powder you already like, choose a store or bulk brand, and buy it on sale. Judge every tub by cost per 25 g of protein, and the expensive ones will explain themselves or get left on the shelf.
          </p>
